===Etymology 1===
{{unk|en}}. {{etydate|1854|nodot=1}} in Pennsylvania as "chebang" in the sense of an {{w|Independent Order of Odd Fellows|Oddfellows lodge}}.<ref name="of">Jack Humphries, "Showing him the 'Chebang'!," ''Raftsman's Journal,'' Clearfield, Pennsylvania, Saturday, July 15, 1854</ref> Attested from the early 1860s with the meaning "inn" and (slightly later) “temporary shelter”. The earliest attestions (1854-1859) are spelled "chebang" and seem abstractly indicate an "affair," "matter of concern," or "happening," in keeping with the modern sense, and seem to be from Midwestern sources; the specific sense of a structure, often pejorative and usually spelled "shebang," seems to originate in the American West just before the Civil War and was widely diffused by troops during the conflict; the sense of a "vehicle” is from 1871–2.<ref name="tow">[http://www.takeourword.com/TOW193/page2.html Take our Word]</ref><ref name="wo">{{R:Wordorigins.org|whole_shebang_the|whole shebang, the|Tuesday, February 20, 2007}}</ref> <ref name="espchb">[https://esnpc.blogspot.com/2015/03/bad-ale-ramshackle-buildings-and-odd.html]</ref> The first two senses seem to have been conflated extensively, though they may have different origins. A note by Massachusetts journalist {{w|Samuel Bowles (journalist)|Samuel Bowles}} dated June 5th, 1865 refers to the term as "vernacular of the [Rocky] Mountains" (Colorado), and defines '''shebang''' as "any kind of an establishment, store, house, shop, shanty."<ref name="sb">Samuel Bowles, "Letter VI: A Sunday in the Mountains" in ''Across the Continent'', Springfield, Mass: Samuel Bowles and Co., 1865, [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Across_the_Continent/HbsTAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=shebang&pg=PA66&printsec=frontcover Across the Continent]</ref> This sense appears in California as early as 1860, "the old '''shebang''' of a theatre."<ref>"Like Old Times," ''The Marysville Appeal,'' Marysville, California, Monday, March 19, 1860</ref> This apparently Western sense is almost certainly from {{m|en|shebeen|shebeen, sheban|cabin where unlicensed liquor is sold and drunk (chiefly in Ireland and Scotland}}, from {{uder|en|ga|síbín||illicit whiskey}}, diminutive of {{m|ga|síob||a drift}}. One of the earliest known quotations, from June 1862 in the Washington Territory, specifically denotes an inn being used as a front for illegal liquor sales.

In the sense of “temporary shelter”, it was perhaps spread by US Civil War Confederate enlistees from Louisiana, from {{uder|en|fr|chabane||hut, cabin}}, a dialectal form of {{uder|en|fr|cabane||a covered hut, lodge, cabin}} (see ''[[cabin]]'', ''[[cabana]]''), or at least influenced by this term. (However, it was ''not'', as sometimes claimed, common among prisoners at {{w|Andersonville National Historic Site|Andersonville}}; [//www.nps.gov/ande/historyculture/myth-shebang.htm the US National Park Service] says it "is virtually absent from most prisoner diaries and contemporary memoirs" and testimony.) The vehicle sense is perhaps from the unrelated {{uder|en|fr|char-à-banc||bus-like wagon with many seats}}. The sense of “matter of concern” could be from either, or onomatopoeia.

